      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-2){e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={y}^{2}-2\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=2y, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}={e}^{2y-1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y=\\int {e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y=\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({y}^{2}-2){e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =({y}^{2}-2)(\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2})(2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}-2){e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\int y\\,{e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int y\\,{e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=y\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}={e}^{2y-1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y=\\int {e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y=\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,{e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(y)(\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2})(1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({y}^{2}-2){e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{({y}^{2}-2){e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\int y\\,{e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}-2){e}^{2y-1}}{2}-(\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}-2){e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y-1}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{4}-D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{4}(2{y}^{2}-2y-3)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}-2){e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{4}(2{y}^{2}-2y-3)]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2(2)-1}}{4}(2(2)^{2}-2(2)-3))-(\\frac{{e}^{2(0)-1}}{4}(2(0)^{2}-2(0)-3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{4}(8-4-3)-\\frac{1}{4e}(0-0-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{4}+\\frac{3}{4e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4}+3}{4e}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
